  <div class="space-y-6">
    <p>
      The <a class="text-sky-400" href={url('/features/ai-rewriting/')}>AI rewriting feature page</a> covers what a
      reader needs to switch the pass on: the per-task toggle, the configuration fields, the presets. This page is the
      layer underneath — the exact REST surface, what a key probe proves, which endpoints the core refuses to call, how
      versions are stored, what the resilience machinery does when a provider is slow or gone, and what the log says
      afterwards. Every statement here was read out of the files named in the section that makes it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
    <p>
      An AI rewrite is not a service you run; it is a stage inside the forwarding pipeline that a task opts into. The
      stage answers four questions and nothing else: <em>should this task rewrite</em>, <em>with which prompt and which
      provider</em>, <em>did the answer survive validation</em>, and <em>what happens to the message when it did
      not</em>. Everything below is one of those four answers.
    </p>
    <p class="not-prose flex flex-wrap gap-2">
      <span class="chip">{aiRoutes} configuration routes parsed from api/main.go</span>
      <span class="chip">{providers.length} provider entries in the catalogue</span>
      <span class="chip">{presets.length} seeded system prompts</span>
      <span class="chip chip-success">{catalogueAi} of {catalogueTotal} feature-catalogue entries mention AI</span>
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Layer', 'File', 'What it owns']}
        rows={[
          ['The stage', '<code>core/application/pipeline.py</code>', 'The gate (<code>ai_config_id</code> plus <code>ai_rewrite_active</code>), prompt selection, the log family and the fallback policy.'],
          ['The transformer', '<code>core/infrastructure/ai/transformer.py</code>', 'The prompt envelope, the timeout, the circuit breaker, failover and output validation.'],
          ['Providers', '<code>core/infrastructure/ai/providers.py</code>', 'The adapter registry, the model families, JSON transport with retries, the credential probe and the endpoint guard.'],
          ['Prompts', '<code>core/application/prompt_service.py</code>', 'Template CRUD, validation, preview, test execution, immutable versions and optimistic concurrency.'],
          ['The surface', '<code>api/main.go</code>', 'Eleven authenticated routes that read and write all of the above.'],
        ]}
        caption="One subsystem, five files — the rest of this page walks them in that order"
      />
    </div>

    <h2 id="surface">The configuration surface</h2>
    <p>
      Eleven routes, all bearer-authenticated, all scoped to the verified tenant, all on <code>/api/</code>. The prompt
      routes deliberately are <em>not</em> under the <code>/api/v1/</code> prefix: that prefix bypasses the bearer
      middleware, and the ownership guard would then have no identity to compare against.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <CodeBlock
        label="bash"
        content={`# What this deployment can call, what it holds, and whether a key works
curl -s localhost:8080/api/ai-configs -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq
curl -s localhost:8080/api/ai-configs/providers -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq
curl -s localhost:8080/api/prompts -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq`}
      />
    </div>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Route', 'What it returns or changes']}
        rows={surface}
        caption="Registered in api/main.go; handlers in api/internal/handlers/handlers.go"
      />
    </div>
    <Callout type="warn" title="One handler exists that no route reaches">
      <p>
        A Go handler for testing a single config in isolation (<code>TestAIRewrite</code>) exists, and the core
        implements the RPC — but no route is registered for it in this checkout, so it is not reachable over HTTP. The
        wired-in test path is <code>POST /api/prompts/test</code>, which takes a prompt and a config together and runs
        the real transform without sending anything.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="providers">Providers and model families</h2>
    <p>
      The provider list is a registry, not a switch statement: an adapter is registered under a name and the rewrite
      path resolves the name out of the same registry, so anything you can pick is something that can be called. Twelve
      vendor entries exist under thirteen keys, because 9Router is registered both as <code>9router</code> (the enum
      spelling) and <code>ninerouter</code> (the alias).
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Provider key', 'Label in the catalogue', 'Model families quoted by the build']}
        rows={providers}
        caption="The factory's MODELS table, served through GET /api/ai-configs/providers"
      />
    </div>
    <ul class="grid gap-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="plug" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">Most vendors speak one protocol.</strong> OpenAI, Groq, DeepSeek, OpenRouter,
          9Router, xAI, Mistral, Together, Fireworks and <code>custom</code> all go through a single OpenAI-compatible
          adapter; Anthropic and Gemini have request shapes of their own. A <code>custom</code> config with no
          <code>base_url</code> is refused outright — there is nothing to call.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="route" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">An empty <code>base_url</code> means "the vendor's own endpoint".</strong> The
          adapter falls back to a per-provider default and appends <code>/v1</code> when the URL you stored carries no
          version suffix. OpenRouter additionally gets the attribution headers this project sets.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="list" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">The model list is curated, not enforced.</strong> It is what the pickers render;
          <code>model</code> is a free string on the wire, so a model released after this build can be typed into a
          config without a code change.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="keys">Key handling</h2>
    <p>
      The key is the one field with special rules, and they matter before an operator swaps one: it is
      <em>write-only</em>. The protobuf says so, the API never echoes it, and the core masks it on the way out.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Property', 'Behaviour']}
        rows={[
          ['At rest', 'Stored in <code>ai_configs.api_key_encrypted</code>, encrypted by the core\u2019s crypto service (AES-256-GCM with a random 12-byte nonce).'],
          ['On every read', 'Masked as <code>***</code> plus the last four characters of the stored key. The value in a <code>GET</code> answer is not a credential you can use, and not the value the operator typed.'],
          ['On update', 'An <code>api_key</code> beginning with <code>***</code> means "unchanged", and the stored key is kept — that is what lets a dashboard re-save a config it can only see masked.'],
          ['On probe', 'The same convention: a blank or masked key means "use the one already stored for that config id".'],
          ['In a refusal', 'A provider message comes back with the key redacted out of it, and the detail is capped at 300 characters — enough to explain a refusal, never the secret.'],
          ['In the response shape', 'The five check-key fields are always present, including a <code>false</code> for <code>success</code>: the answer is projected field by field rather than marshalled from the protobuf, so a client never has to read a missing field as a refusal.'],
        ]}
        caption="api/internal/handlers/handlers.go, the core servicer and core/infrastructure/security/crypto.py"
      />
    </div>
    <Callout type="info" title="A masked value is evidence, not a value">
      <p>
        Because reads return <code>***9f2c</code>, a client can prove which key is stored without holding it. Sending
        that value anywhere except an update or a probe sends a non-credential — treat <code>***…</code> as a
        fingerprint.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="check-key">check-key, the pre-save probe</h2>
    <p>
      A broken key that has been stored is worse than one that was refused: the config looks healthy in the panel and
      every message pays for it. <code>POST /api/ai-configs/check-key</code> is the gate in front of that. It is
      deliberately not a rewrite — it asks for the cheapest call the provider offers.
    </p>
    <Steps
      steps={[
        {
          title: 'Ownership first',
          body: 'A named <code>ai_config_id</code> must belong to the caller, because the probe would spend that config\u2019s credentials. A foreign or unknown id answers <code>config_not_found</code> — as a refusal, not an error.',
        },
        {
          title: 'Blanks are filled from the stored config',
          body: 'Whatever the caller leaves out is taken from the config it named, so a UI can re-check a saved key without ever receiving it. A masked key counts as "leave it as stored".',
        },
        {
          title: 'The request is assembled per provider',
          body: 'One token, temperature 0: OpenAI-compatible providers call <code>/chat/completions</code>, Anthropic calls <code>/messages</code>, Gemini calls <code>:generateContent</code>. The probe budget is 12 seconds and redirects are not followed.',
          code: 'POST <base>/chat/completions   {"model": "…", "max_tokens": 1, "messages": [{"role":"user","content":"ping"}]}',
        },
        {
          title: 'The endpoint guard runs before any socket is opened',
          body: 'The URL the tenant typed is a request the core makes from inside your network, so it is checked first — with the name resolved, not merely parsed.',
        },
        {
          title: 'The answer is classified, then redacted',
          body: 'A 2xx means the credentials work. 401/403 is <code>invalid_key</code>, 404 <code>endpoint_not_found</code>, 429 <code>rate_limited</code>, 5xx <code>provider_error</code>. One quirk is handled: a 400 that mentions <code>max_tokens</code> is retried once with <code>max_completion_tokens</code>, so a newer model is not reported as a broken key.',
        },
      ]}
    />
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['error_code', 'What it means']}
        rows={probeCodes}
        caption="The probe reports a status class, never a provider body verbatim"
      />
    </div>
    <Callout type="tip" title="Read two fields, not one">
      <p>
        <code>success</code> means "the probe ran and reached an answer"; <code>healthy</code> means "the endpoint
        accepted these credentials". A guard refusal reports <code>success=false</code> precisely so it is never
        mistaken for a provider outage — nothing left the process.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="guard">The endpoint guard</h2>
    <p>
      A tenant-supplied URL is a server-side request. Left unchecked it is a port scanner with a status code in the
      reply, aimed at whatever network the core happens to live in. The guard exists for that, and it runs twice: at the
      probe (with DNS resolution) and again at every write, because a stored endpoint is replayed on every rewrite
      afterwards.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Verdict', 'Triggered by']}
        rows={guardVerdicts}
        caption="core/infrastructure/ai/providers.py — endpoint_guard and _allow_private_endpoints"
      />
    </div>
    <ul class="grid gap-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="lock" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-violet-300 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">Private model servers are opt-in.</strong> Set
          <code>ATF_ALLOW_PRIVATE_AI_ENDPOINTS</code> to <code>1</code>, <code>true</code>, <code>yes</code> or
          <code>on</code> on the core and a LAN address becomes callable. Every other row of the table still applies, so
          the switch widens one class of address and nothing more.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="eye" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">One bad answer is enough.</strong> When the guard resolves a name it inspects
          every address it resolves to — a hostname answering with both a public and a private address is refused, not
          sampled.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="alert" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-amber-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">Know what the write-time check does not do.</strong> The probe resolves names;
          the create and update path calls the same guard without a resolver, so it is a syntax, hostname and
          literal-address check. A name that resolves into private space is therefore refused at probe time, while a
          literal private address is refused at both. A refused write surfaces as gRPC
          <code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code> carrying the guard code, so the API answers <code>400</code> with something a
          client can translate instead of a generic server error.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="shield" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">The redirect is not followed.</strong> A provider that answers with a redirect
          is classified on the redirect itself, so a probed name cannot hand the request to a second host the guard
          never saw.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="prompts">Prompts and version history</h2>
    <p>
      A prompt template is a first-class record, not a text box: name, description, system prompt, user template,
      target language and a version counter. Four system presets are seeded on first start, each with version 1 recorded
      as "Initial system default version". They are shared across tenants, while a tenant's own templates are visible
      only to that account.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Template id', 'Name', 'Instruction the model receives']}
        rows={presets}
        caption="core/application/prompt_service.py — DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPTS"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Rule', 'Detail']}
        rows={[
          ['System prompts are immutable', 'They cannot be edited and cannot be deleted: the API refuses a body claiming <code>is_system</code> with <code>403 system_prompt_immutable</code>, and the core refuses a write to a system template even if the wire allowed it through.'],
          ['Validation is explicit', 'The system prompt may not be empty and may not exceed <code>8000</code> characters; the user template may not exceed <code>4000</code>, must contain the <code>&#123;text&#125;</code> placeholder, and may contain no other placeholder.'],
          ['Every save is a new version', 'An update appends an immutable version row with a change summary and increments <code>current_version</code>; the previous text stays readable by version number.'],
          ['Concurrency is optimistic', 'A write naming an <code>expected_version</code> that no longer matches is refused as a conflict rather than merged, so two editors cannot silently overwrite each other.'],
          ['Pinning is per task', 'A task stores a prompt id and optionally a version, and the pipeline resolves exactly that pair — a prompt can be improved for everyone while one task keeps the wording it was tuned on.'],
        ]}
        caption="Behaviour read from core/application/prompt_service.py and the AI control servicer"
      />
    </div>
    <Callout type="warn" title="Rollback is implemented but not routed">
      <p>
        The prompt service can roll a template back: it copies the target version's text forward as a new version with
        the summary "Rolled back to vN", which keeps the history linear instead of rewriting it. No route, RPC or CLI
        verb calls it in this checkout, and no route lists version history either — <code>GET /api/prompts</code>
        returns the current version number. To roll back from a client, read the text you want and <code>PUT</code> it
        with the version you got; to see the whole history, read the store directly.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="runtime">What runs per message</h2>
    <p>
      The order matters more than the individual steps, because two of them are decisions about the same text. This is
      the sequence for one message on a task with a rewrite attached.
    </p>
    <Steps
      steps={[
        {
          title: 'Content policy transforms first',
          body: 'Per-task content rules rewrite the text before the model is asked, so the model never sees text the task was already going to strip and a rewrite cannot resurrect content a rule removed.',
        },
        {
          title: 'The gate answers one question',
          body: 'A task rewrites when it has an <code>ai_config_id</code> and its <code>ai_rewrite_active</code> property is true — which it is unless the task metadata carries <code>paid_addons.ai_rewrite</code> and that value is false. When the gate is shut, the pipeline logs the off-path line and forwards the text unmodified; it does not treat the task as broken.',
          code: '[PIPELINE:AI_REWRITE:OFF] [7f3a91c2] Rule 3f1c9a2e keeps cfg=9a11c7f0 but rewrite is disabled (smart_ai_rewrite=False, paid_addons=False) — delivering unmodified text',
        },
        {
          title: 'The prompt is resolved',
          body: 'The task\u2019s prompt id, and an optional pinned version, is rendered by the prompt service; without a prompt id the AI config\u2019s own <code>system_prompt</code> is used, and an empty one falls back to a plain instruction to rewrite cleanly. A prompt id that cannot be resolved is logged and the request continues with the config\u2019s prompt rather than failing the message.',
        },
        {
          title: 'The request is shaped for the message type',
          body: 'A message with media is treated as a caption: a <code>1024</code>-character budget instead of <code>4096</code>. The timeout comes from the task (<code>ai_timeout_seconds</code>, default <code>15</code>, clamped between <code>1</code> and <code>120</code>), and the target language is passed as <code>auto</code>.',
        },
        {
          title: 'The transform runs under a deadline',
          body: 'The call is wrapped in a timeout, goes through the circuit breaker, and its answer is validated before anything is sent. A validated answer replaces the text; anything else hands over to the fallback policy.',
        },
        {
          title: 'The tail of the pipeline still runs',
          body: 'Final replacements, then header and footer, then the advanced content transforms — and the transformed text is capped again at delivery. A rewrite cannot smuggle text past the header/footer stage or past the cap.',
        },
      ]}
    />
    <Callout type="info" title="Two per-task AI settings the rewrite path does not read">
      <p>
        The settings registry declares <code>ai_creativity</code> (default <code>0.7</code>) and
        <code>ai_max_tokens</code> (default <code>600</code>), and the task editors render them. No code in the rewrite
        path reads either: the request sends the <code>temperature</code> of the AI config, and the output cap is the
        validator's caption/text limit. Set creativity on the config, not on the task.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="envelope">The prompt envelope</h2>
    <p>
      What the model receives is not your system prompt on its own. It is a fixed security preamble, then your
      instructions, then the message — wrapped in tags the model is told to treat as data:
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <CodeBlock label="The envelope, as assembled" language="text" content={envelope} />
    </div>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Element', 'Where it comes from']}
        rows={[
          ['The security preamble', 'A constant in the transformer: five numbered rules that declare the message passive data, forbid following instructions inside it, and forbid echoing the tags.'],
          ['<code>Instructions:</code>', 'The pinned template\u2019s system prompt when a task pins one, otherwise the AI config\u2019s <code>system_prompt</code>, otherwise a built-in fallback sentence.'],
          ['<code>Target Language:</code>', 'Taken from the request, which the pipeline sets to <code>auto</code>. A template\u2019s own language field is used when a template is previewed or tested, not when the pipeline calls it.'],
          ['The user prompt', 'The template with <code>&#123;text&#125;</code> replaced by the message. When a pinned prompt is in play the rendered prompt already contains the message, so the raw text is appended after it rather than substituted into it.'],
          ['<code>Transformed Result:</code>', 'A closing cue; the model is asked to return only the transformed text.'],
        ]}
        caption="core/infrastructure/ai/transformer.py — _build_prompt_payload"
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      The envelope defends against injection; the validator defends against a model that did it anyway, and against a
      reply that would break Telegram. Every success path passes through it:
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Check', 'Behaviour']}
        rows={validatorChecks}
        caption="core/infrastructure/ai/validator.py — a failed check counts as a transform failure"
      />
    </div>

    <h2 id="resilience">Circuit breaker and failover</h2>
    <p>
      A task may name a second AI config. The transformer then asks the circuit registry which one to use, and the
      answer is deterministic: the primary if its breaker allows it, otherwise the secondary — never both in parallel,
      and never another attempt at the same dead config.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Decision', 'Result']}
        rows={[
          ['The primary circuit allows a call', 'The primary is used, and <code>failover</code> is false in the success log line.'],
          ['The primary is open; a secondary is named and its circuit allows a call', 'The secondary is used and the result is flagged as failover. If the secondary turns out to be missing or disabled, the transformer falls back to the primary for that attempt rather than failing the message over a configuration detail.'],
          ['Both are open, or no secondary is named', 'The attempt is refused with <code>CIRCUIT_OPEN</code> and marked retryable. The provider is not called at all — which is the point of the breaker.'],
        ]}
        caption="core/infrastructure/ai/circuit_breaker.py — select_healthy_provider"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['State', 'Meaning']}
        rows={[
          ['<code>CLOSED</code>', 'Normal. Every request is allowed.'],
          ['<code>OPEN</code>', 'After <code>3</code> consecutive failures the circuit trips and refuses to call that config until the recovery window has passed.'],
          ['<code>HALF_OPEN</code>', 'Reached lazily once <code>30</code> seconds have elapsed since the last failure: the next call is a probe. One success closes the circuit; a failure re-opens it.'],
          ['Registry', 'Circuits are created on demand and keyed by the id the caller passes. The pipeline passes AI config ids, so a breaker tracks a configuration rather than a vendor.'],
        ]}
        caption="Defaults: failure_threshold 3, recovery_timeout 30s"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['error_code', 'Retryable?', 'Raised when']}
        rows={errorCodes}
        caption="The codes a task's fallback policy reacts to"
      />
    </div>
    <ul class="grid gap-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="refresh" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">Retries happen below the breaker, not through it.</strong> One attempt gets 45
          seconds of network budget, and a transient status (408, 425, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504) or a timeout is retried
          twice inside the same call the breaker is timing.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="gauge" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">A configuration trips, not a vendor.</strong> The registry hands out one
          breaker per id it is given. Two configs pointed at the same vendor keep independent state, and the breaker
          that records an outcome is the one belonging to the config actually used.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="info" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">Read the signature, not the intent.</strong>
          <code>record_failure(is_transient=…)</code> accepts whether a failure was transient, and the callers answer
          honestly — but the state machine increments the failure counter either way and does not consult the flag. A
          non-transient <code>401</code> counts toward the same threshold of 3 as a timeout.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="clock" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">Recovery is lazy and free.</strong> Nothing schedules a timer: the state is
          re-evaluated when someone asks, so an open circuit costs nothing while idle and the first rewrite after the
          window is the probe that decides whether to close it again.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="when-down">When the provider is down</h2>
    <p>
      A failed transform is a per-task decision, not a global setting, and the shipped default is the strict one. The
      task field is <code>ai_fallback_policy</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Policy', 'What happens to the message']}
        rows={fallbacks}
        caption="core/application/pipeline.py — _handle_ai_fallback"
      />
    </div>
    <Callout type="warn" title="The default fails closed, and that is worth knowing before an outage">
      <p>
        With no policy set, a task uses <code>DROP</code>: the message is not delivered. Only
        <code>SEND_ORIGINAL</code> forwards the untransformed text. Two further consequences of reading the code rather
        than the intent: <code>RETRY</code> and <code>QUARANTINE</code> both return "no text" from the transform stage,
        so in this checkout neither delivers the message, and no code in the forwarding path reads the
        <code>ai_retry:</code> / <code>ai_quarantine:</code> stage reasons — there is no requeue and no quarantine store
        to find them later. If a rewrite is a nice-to-have, say <code>SEND_ORIGINAL</code> explicitly.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p>
      The drop happens early, too: the transform stage runs before a delivery job is enqueued, so a message dropped by
      the fallback policy never becomes a queue entry — the retry budget and the dead-letter list never see it. The
      evidence is the log line and the recorded transform stage, and that is all.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <CodeBlock
        label="Example transcript — reconstructed from the source's log format strings, not captured from a live run"
        language="text"
        content={transcript}
      />
    </div>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <CodeBlock
        label="Choosing the policy, and taking the provider out of the way"
        content={`# Make delivery independent of the provider
atf rules update news-mirror --set ai_fallback_policy=SEND_ORIGINAL

# Or switch the pass off entirely, keeping the configuration attached
atf rules settings set news-mirror paid_addons.ai_rewrite=false`}
      />
    </div>

    <h2 id="observability">Logs and evidence</h2>
    <p>
      The stage has its own log family, and the prefixes are the fastest way to tell "the rewrite was switched off"
      apart from "the provider failed" — two problems with very different fixes.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Prefix', 'What the line tells you']}
        rows={logPrefixes}
        caption="core/application/pipeline.py, plus the bot UI's toggle record"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="not-prose my-4">
      <CodeBlock label="bash" content={logs} />
    </div>
    <ul class="grid gap-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="chip" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">There is no AI health endpoint.</strong> The provider is a third-party
          dependency the stack does not own, so the deployment-level check is <code>atf doctor</code>'s "ai backend"
          line: it reads <code>AI_BASE_URL</code>/<code>OPENAI_BASE_URL</code> from the environment, reports reachable
          or unreachable, and says "not configured (AI rewrite disabled)" when neither is set. It cannot see per-tenant
          configs; use <code>check-key</code> for those.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="activity" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-sky-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.2} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">Counters are in-process, not published.</strong> The pipeline keeps totals for
          transforms started, succeeded, failed and per fallback policy, and the transformer keeps its own request,
          failover, timeout and validation counters. No REST route exposes them in this checkout — the observable
          evidence is the log family above, the circuit-state transitions, and each task's delivery history.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-emerald-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.4} />
        <span>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">Every line carries the correlation id.</strong> The rewrite lines open with the
          pipeline's short correlation prefix, so an API call, its gRPC exchange and the rewrite it caused join on one
          id — the same contract the rest of the stack uses.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="catalogue">Where this stands</h2>
    <p>
      The project keeps its own status list in <code>docs/feature_catalog.json</code>: {catalogueTotal} entries
      generated from a source inspection, each carrying an <code>implementation_status</code> ({repoInfo.featureCatalogStatus.implemented}{' '}
      implemented, {repoInfo.featureCatalogStatus.partial} partial, {repoInfo.featureCatalogStatus.planned} planned). AI
      rewriting is <strong>not one of them</strong> — no entry in that catalogue covers it, so this page claims no
      catalogue status for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      What it claims instead is narrower and checkable: the behaviour described here is implemented in the files named in
      each section. Where something is absent — the unrouted test handler, the unrouted rollback service, the missing
      version-history route, the per-task AI settings the rewrite path never reads — it is called out as absent rather
      than rounded up. Treat this as documented-in-code behaviour, and the catalogue as the list of features the project
      formally tracks.
    </p>

    <h2 id="examples">Worked examples</h2>
    <p>
      Three sequences: prove and store a credential, attach it to a task with the policy you want, then own a prompt
      template through a test and an edit.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose space-y-4">
      {examples.map((example) => <CodeBlock label={example.label} content={example.content} />)}
    </div>
    <Callout type="tip" title="The interfaces are the other way in">
      <p>
        Everything the REST surface does is reachable from the shipped clients: the Telegram bot has an AI menu that
        lists configs and starts the add flow, the dashboard renders the provider catalogue and the prompt templates,
        and the mobile app calls the same <code>check-key</code> route before saving. The differences are convenience,
        not capability, and the clients share one server-side catalogue so they cannot drift apart.
      </p>
    </Callout>

